{"id":"84827cb2-e3d7-48bc-a2ba-15e347a0a6fa","object":"chat.completion","created":1785026361,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        n_copy = dict(n)\\n        n_copy['children'] = []\\n        node_map[n_copy['id']] = n_copy\\n    for n in node_map.values():\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None and p in node_map:\\n            node_map[p]['children'].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for n in node_map.values():\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    def subtree_ids(node_id):\\n        ids = [node_id]\\n        for child_id in node_map[node_id]['children']:\\n            if child_id not in removed:\\n                ids.extend(subtree_ids(child_id))\\n        return ids\\n\\n    def compute_delta(sub_ids):\\n        delta = {}\\n        for nid in sub_ids:\\n            sym = node_map[nid]['symbol']\\n            delta[sym] = delta.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        return delta\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == 'root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        children_retained = [c for c in node['children'] if c not in removed]\\n        if children_retained:\\n            continue\\n        sub_ids = subtree_ids(nid)\\n        delta = compute_delta(sub_ids)\\n        feasible = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if symbol_count.get(sym, 0) - delta.get(sym, 0) <= 0:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n        if feasible:\\n            for sid in sub_ids:\\n                removed.add(sid)\\n                symbol_count[node_map[sid]['symbol']] -= 1\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in node_map if nid not in removed])\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that processes a tree of nodes, each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The task is to perform \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" which appears to be a process of removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols, and respecting tree structure (cannot remove a node if it has retained children? Actually typical Perses approach: you can remove a node if its children are moved up or something? But the description says: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node that still has children that we are keeping. That implies that removal is done bottom-up: we must remove children first (or not remove node if children are retained). The objective: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes, but we must ensure that all required_symbols are still present after removals. Also deeper nodes before shallower nodes. Use node ID as stable tie break. Return `removed_node_ids` (order of removal) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably alphabetically or by something? The schema says array of strings, no specific sort order required but they say sorted retained IDs. Probably sort by ID string lexicographically). Also removal order: the order in which nodes were removed (presumably the order we decide to remove them). The algorithm: \n- Start with all nodes.\n- Identify nodes that are removable and whose removal does not violate required symbols. But we also must consider that if a node is removed, its children might become orphaned? Actually in parse tree reduction, when you remove a node, you typically replace it with its children (or with a subtree). But the description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests that when you remove a node, you cannot have children that are retained because they would be orphans. So you must either remove all descendants or not remove the node. The typical grammar-guided reduction (like Perses) reduces the tree by removing subtrees that are not needed. But here we have a simpler scenario: a tree where each node is removable or not, and we want to minimize the tree while keeping required symbols. The rule \"remove deeper before shallower\" suggests we traverse nodes in decreasing depth, and for each removable node, we check if it can be removed (i.e., its symbol not required, root not removed, and no retained children). If so, we remove it and all its descendants? Actually if we remove a node, its descendants become irrelevant because they are not reachable? But the problem states \"retained_node_ids\" are the nodes that remain after all removals. So we need to compute the set of nodes that are kept. \n\nGiven the constraints, a plausible algorithm:\n- Build a tree structure from nodes (id -> node dict with children list).\n- Identify all node ids.\n- required_symbols: set of symbols that must be represented by at least one node in the final retained set. (Note: root symbol \"root\" is always required).\n- We need to decide which nodes to remove. The condition \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" implies that if we want to remove a node, we must also remove all its children (i.e., entire subtree). Otherwise, the children would be retained but parent removed, making them rootless? But that would be invalid. So removal of a node implies removal of its entire subtree. Therefore, the set of retained nodes must form a connected subtree containing the root and all required symbols. This is essentially a Steiner tree problem but simpler due to hierarchical constraints.\n\nGiven \"required_symbols\", we need to keep at least one node for each required symbol. Since the tree is a parse tree, symbols may repeat. We need to keep nodes such that all required symbols are present among retained nodes. The root is always retained (non-removable). Then we can optionally prune removable subtrees as long as we don't remove needed symbols. We prefer to remove deeper nodes first. So we can traverse nodes in decreasing depth, and for each node that is removable and whose removal would not cause loss of a required symbol (i.e., the required symbols are still present elsewhere in the tree), we can remove it and its entire subtree. But we must also ensure that we don't remove a node if any of its ancestors are removed (since removal is bottom-up). Actually if we remove node A, all its descendants are gone. If later we consider node B (ancestor of A) that is removable, it might be that all descendants of B are already removed, so removing B would also be fine? But we already removed A; B's children are already removed, so B has no retained children, so we can remove B as well. The order \"remove deeper before shallower\" ensures we consider deeper nodes first. So algorithm: sort nodes by depth descending, then stable tie break by id. For each node in that order, if it is removable (node[\"removable\"]==True), and its id is not the root, and its symbol is not in required_symbols (or if required symbol appears elsewhere? Actually we must check: after removal of that node and its subtree, do all required symbols still exist in the remaining tree? That is the key condition. So we need to compute the current set of retained nodes. Start with all nodes. When we tentatively remove a node, we simulate removal of its entire subtree. But we must also consider that later we might remove more nodes. We need to decide the final set. This is similar to greedy pruning: for each node in decreasing depth, if removing it (and its subtree) does not cause any required symbol to disappear, we remove it. This is a typical approach.\n\nBut we must also ensure that we don't remove a node that has retained children (i.e., if we haven't yet removed all its children). Since we process deeper first, by the time we consider a node, all its descendants have been considered (and possibly removed). So if we decide to keep any descendant (because it's non-removable or required), then the current node cannot be removed because it has retained children. That aligns with \"node with retained children\". So in the algorithm, when we consider a node, we check if any of its children are currently retained (i.e., not removed yet). If yes, skip. Otherwise, we can consider removal.\n\nWe also need to check required symbols. We need a counter of how many nodes of each symbol are retained. We can maintain a multiset. When we remove a subtree, we decrement counts for all symbols in that subtree. The condition for removal is that after removal, every required symbol still has count > 0.\n\nEdge case: What if a required symbol node is itself removable? According to description, \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if its symbol is required, even if there are other nodes with same symbol? The phrase \"required symbol\" likely means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So such nodes must be retained regardless. So we should not attempt to remove them. So we need to check: if node's symbol in required_symbols, skip removal.\n\nAlso ensure root is never removed.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Input: case: dict with \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings).\n- Build:\n  - node_map: id -> node dict (add children list)\n  - For each node, parent_id.\n  - Build children lists.\n  - Compute depth from root? It's already given.\n- Initialize set of retained node ids to all node ids.\n- Initialize symbol_count: dict from symbol to number of nodes with that symbol currently retained.\n- Also we may need to track which nodes are removed (and their descendants). We'll have a removed set.\n- Sort nodes in order of decreasing depth, then by id for tie-break.\n- For each node in that order:\n  - if node['id'] == 'root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove)\n  - if not node['removable']: skip\n  - Check if any child of node is still retained (i.e., not removed). Because if child retained, cannot remove node. (Need to check children list).\n  - If children retained, skip.\n  - Simulate removal: compute total symbols from the subtree rooted at this node that would be removed (including node itself). But we need to ensure that for each required symbol, the count after removal remains >0. Since we are processing deeper first, the subtree may already have some nodes removed previously? Actually if we process deeper first, any child of this node would have been processed earlier (since deeper). If we removed a child, that child's subtree is gone, but the parent still has a child? Actually if we removed a child, then the parent's child link is effectively broken. But we haven't removed the parent node, so the parent still has an entry in children list but that child is removed. In our data structure, we should treat removed nodes as absent. So when we check \"any child retained\", we mean any child that is not removed. So we need to maintain a set of removed ids. So when we process a node, we need to know which children are currently retained. That's fine.\n  - But also when simulating subtree removal, we need to count symbols only in the currently retained part of the subtree. Because some descendants may already be removed. However, if we are processing